# gate-remediation-deny's starts of core/hooks/ai-dlc-gate-remediation-guard.sh (BL-879 grammar). Every other drive is
# in process (run.sh drive(): inproc_load once, inproc_run grg_main per call; receipt-bl989.sh, which run.sh starts as
# its own program, loads the hook itself and runs it in process). The hook exits 0 on every path, so the cli cells are
# its two outcomes (a deny decision on stdout; a silent allow), each started as FXCELL=<id> bash "$HOOK" on a copy of a
# seeded workspace and compared with the in-process call on another copy: stdout, stderr, exit and the normalised
# workspace tree.
cli core/hooks/ai-dlc-gate-remediation-guard.sh cli-deny
cli core/hooks/ai-dlc-gate-remediation-guard.sh cli-allow
